<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carmelites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Order of Carmelites, Carmelite Order</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carmelites are a Catholic religious order that began among hermits on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in the late twelfth century and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as the crusader states declined. In sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross">John of the Cross</a> led a reform that returned to strict enclosure, poverty and prayer, creating the Discalced Carmelites, recognised as a separate order in 1593. Both reformers became celebrated mystical writers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carmelites.jpg" alt="Carmelites" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elias%27_Himmelfahrt-DSC2080.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Historical records of the order's origin are uncertain. Berthold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and Albert of Vercelli are traditionally associated with its founding, but few clear records survive. Tradition gives 1185, but that rests on a pilgrim's story whose interpretation is questionable; the oldest reliable written accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hermits on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a> date from 1220 and 1263. The Carmelites look back not to a founder but to the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> and his disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a>, whom they regard as their spiritual fathers. A written rule of life for the hermits, centred on prayer, is dated 1209 and was requested, tradition says, from the Latin Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, Albert of Vercelli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the hermits met difficulties. Their solitary life did not suit their new settlements, and they competed with other mendicant orders. In 1274 the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> disestablished all mendicant orders founded after 1215; only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, the Order of Preachers, the Carmelites and the Augustinians remained. The Carmelites, barely spared, had to change from hermits to friars. They settled across Europe, among other places at Aylesford in Kent in 1242 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1259.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> had reduced the number of members, a majority of Carmelites asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> in 1432 to mitigate the rule because of its severity. John Soreth, Prior General from 1451 to 1471, worked to restore a stricter life. The Teresian reform of the 1560s created the Discalced Carmelites, who faced opposition from unreformed houses: Carmelites at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross">John of the Cross</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> suppressed the order, and the Compiègne Carmelites were guillotined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Carmelites are a mendicant order for men and women. The order of Carmelite nuns was formalised in 1452, and John Soreth had a leading part in founding the Third Order for lay members. Their distinctive garment is a scapular of two strips of dark cloth worn on the breast and back. Tradition holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> gave it to Simon Stock and promised that those who die wearing it with faith and piety would be saved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carmelites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the chief power of the Catholic Counter-Reformation: Ignatius of Loyola founded the Society of Jesus, Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross reformed the <b>Carmelites</b>, and Spanish theologians shaped the Council of Trent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's engagement with Christianity began almost with its independence, when Diogo I of Kongo sent missionaries; <b>Carmelites</b> in 1583 and Jesuits in 1603 and 1628 reported conversions, and the Capuchin baptism of 1663 brought in 6,000 subjects before civil war defeated the Christian party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross, Spanish mystics and reformers of the <b>Carmelite Order</b>, taught interior conversion and deeper prayer; Thomas Merton called John of the Cross the greatest of all mystical theologians.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
